          "This module classifies repository files and, for tabular data files available locally, extracts each column's type and summary statistics.",
          "#### Data Tree",
          "Where the files sit within each repository. Each file is followed by how it was classified, which study it belongs to.",
          "**Repository: https://osf.io/int64**",
          "```\n└── survey.csv  📊 data ex1\n```",
          "#### Data Files",
          "::: {.panel-tabset}\n\n## survey.csv\n\n\n```{r}\n#| echo: false\n\n\n# table data --------------------------------------\ntable <- structure(list(participant = structure(c(9.88131291687434e-313, \n1.48219693753362e-312, 1.97626258336499e-312, 4.94065645861009e-313, \n9.88131291707196e-313, 1.48219693752374e-312, 1.97626258339957e-312, \n4.94065645880772e-313, 9.88131291682493e-313, 1.48219693757315e-312, \n1.97626258341933e-312, 4.94065645841247e-313, 9.88131291746722e-313, \n1.48219693759291e-312, 1.97626258336499e-312, 4.94065645920297e-313, \n9.88131291766484e-313, 1.48219693752374e-312, 1.97626258345886e-312, \n4.9406564594006e-313, 9.88131291682493e-313, 1.48219693763243e-312, \n1.97626258347862e-312, 4.94065645841247e-313, 9.88131291806009e-313, \n1.4821969376522e-312, 1.97626258336499e-312, 4.94065645979585e-313, \n9.88131291825772e-313, 1.48219693752374e-312, 1.97626258351815e-312, \n4.94065645999348e-313, 9.88131291682493e-313, 1.48219693769172e-312, \n1.97626258353791e-312), class = \"integer64\"), age = 19:53, item_1 = c(4L, \n3L, 4L, 3L, 1L, 3L, 1L, 2L, 4L, 5L, 2L, 1L, 1L, 5L, 4L, 1L, 2L, \n1L, 1L, 3L, 5L, 3L, 4L, 3L, 2L, 5L, 2L, 3L, 2L, 1L, 3L, 1L, 4L, \n1L, 4L), item_2 = c(4L, 3L, 1L, 3L, 3L, 1L, 4L, 4L, 4L, 3L, 1L, \n4L, 2L, 3L, 5L, 4L, 4L, 1L, 3L, 3L, 4L, 4L, 4L, 3L, 5L, 3L, 3L, \n2L, 2L, 3L, 4L, 2L, 5L, 1L, 3L), item_3 = c(1L, 3L, 1L, 3L, 5L, \n4L, 1L, 4L, 5L, 4L, 2L, 5L, 4L, 2L, 4L, 4L, 2L, 1L, 5L, 3L, 2L, \n1L, 3L, 4L, 3L, 1L, 2L, 5L, 5L, 4L, 3L, 4L, 5L, 4L, 1L), item_4 = c(1L, \n5L, 3L, 3L, 4L, 3L, 2L, 4L, 3L, 3L, 2L, 2L, 5L, 5L, 4L, 1L, 1L, \n5L, 1L, 3L, 3L, 1L, 1L, 2L, 2L, 3L, 5L, 2L, 4L, 3L, 3L, 2L, 1L, \n2L, 4L), item_5 = c(5L, 4L, 4L, 3L, 3L, 2L, 3L, 1L, 2L, 4L, 2L, \n3L, 3L, 5L, 4L, 2L, 3L, 2L, 1L, 3L, 3L, 4L, 2L, 5L, 5L, 5L, 5L, \n2L, 3L, 2L, 1L, 3L, 4L, 4L, 4L), item_6 = c(5L, 5L, 1L, 3L, 4L, \n5L, 2L, 2L, 4L, 2L, 2L, 3L, 5L, 1L, 4L, 1L, 5L, 5L, 2L, 3L, 5L, \n4L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 3L, 5L, 4L, 1L, 5L, 2L, 2L, 3L, 2L, 4L)), row.names = c(NA, \n35L), class = \"data.frame\")\n\n# display table -----------------------------------\nmetacheck::report_table(table, \"auto\", 10, FALSE)\n```\n\n\n:::",
          "#### Descriptives Overview\n\nWe found 0 columns with at least one missing value.",
          "::: {.panel-tabset}\n\n## survey.csv\n\n\n```{r}\n#| echo: false\n\n\n# table data --------------------------------------\ntable <- structure(list(Column = c(\"age\", \"item_1\", \"item_2\", \"item_3\", \n\"item_4\", \"item_5\", \"item_6\", \"participant\"), Representation = c(\"numeric\", \n\"numeric\", \"numeric\", \"numeric\", \"numeric\", \"numeric\", \"numeric\", \n\"text\"), Level = c(\"ratio\", \"ordinal\", \"ordinal\", \"ordinal\", \n\"ordinal\", \"ordinal\", \"ordinal\", \"nominal\"), Concept = c(\"age\", \n\"likert\", \"likert\", \"likert\", \"likert\", \"likert\", \"likert\", \"id\"\n), Role = c(\"measure\", \"measure\", \"measure\", \"measure\", \"measure\", \n\"measure\", \"measure\", \"identifier\"), Unit = c(\"years\", \"\", \"\", \n\"\", \"\", \"\", \"\", \"\"), Rows = c(35L, 35L, 35L, 35L, 35L, 35L, 35L, \n35L), \"% Missing\" = c(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0), \"N Unique\" = c(35L, \n5L, 5L, 5L, 5L, 5L, 5L, 28L), Mean = c(36, 2.686, 3.086, 3.143, \n2.8, 3.171, 3.057, NA), SD = c(10.247, 1.388, 1.173, 1.438, 1.324, \n1.224, 1.514, NA), Min = c(19, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, NA), Max = c(53, \n5, 5, 5, 5, 5, 5, NA)), row.names = c(\"age\", \"item_1\", \"item_2\", \n\"item_3\", \"item_4\", \"item_5\", \"item_6\", \"participant\"), class = \"data.frame\")\n\n# display table -----------------------------------\nmetacheck::report_table(table, \"auto\", 25, FALSE)\n```\n\n\n:::",
          "This module runs automated data-quality checks (outliers, miscoded missing values, empty and constant columns, SPSS filter variables, category casing, problematic column names, mixed text encodings, spreadsheet formatting) on the extracted data files.",
          "We examined 8 columns across 1 data file.",
          "#### Careless Responding",
          "2 distinct respondents were flagged for **straightlining**: giving the same answer for at least 80% of the items in a multi-item scale of 5 items or more. One respondent can be flagged in several scales; the table below is **one row per person**, and the *Longest run* column gives the run that triggered the strongest flag, so you can check it against the raw data.",
          "Of these, **2 were flagged *only* by short-scale straightlining** (a run of identical answers on a scale of 7 items or fewer). On a short, one-directional scale, answering consistently is often normal, coherent responding rather than carelessness — treat these as weak signals and inspect the actual responses before excluding anyone.",
          "These are prompts to inspect those rows, not definitive judgements.",
          "\n```{r}\n#| echo: false\n\n\n# table data --------------------------------------\ntable <- structure(list(Respondent = c(\"100000000004\", \"100000000020\"), \n    \"Blocks flagged\" = c(1L, 1L), Scales = c(\"item (1-5, 6 items)\", \n    \"item (1-5, 6 items)\"), \"Longest run\" = c(\"same answer 6 times in a row, out of 6 items (flagged at 5)\", \n    \"same answer 6 times in a row, out of 6 items (flagged at 5)\"\n    ), \"Short-scale only\" = c(\"yes\", \"yes\")), row.names = 1:2, class = \"data.frame\")\n\n# display table -----------------------------------\nmetacheck::report_table(table, \"auto\", 20, FALSE)\n```\n",
          "**What this does not cover.** Even in a screened file, these checks only look at multi-item rating scales — the only place where a repeated or flat answer pattern is interpretable. Single questions, open text, demographics and any item outside a detected scale block are never examined. A respondent can therefore answer most of a survey carelessly and still not be flagged here, and finding nothing is not evidence that a dataset is free of careless responding."
        ]
